<p class="gna-lede">Most people go home on the day of TMJ arthroscopy or the morning after, return to desk work within about a week, and move from a soft diet back to normal food over four to six weeks. The full benefit — less pain and better mouth opening — usually builds over three to six months and depends heavily on doing your jaw exercises. Recovery varies from person to person, so treat the timeline below as a guide rather than a rulebook.</p>

<h2 id="short-answer">How long does TMJ arthroscopy recovery take?</h2>
<p>TMJ arthroscopy is a keyhole procedure on the jaw joint (the temporomandibular joint, just in front of the ear). It is done under general anaesthesia through one or two small punctures in the skin. Because there is no large incision, recovery is much quicker than after open joint surgery. Even so, the joint has been washed out, stretched and sometimes treated from the inside, and the surrounding tissues need time to settle.</p>
<p>It helps to think about recovery in three phases: the first few days (swelling and stiffness), the first month (gradually getting movement and diet back) and the first three to six months (the joint lining healing and the result becoming clear). For background on the procedure itself, our page on <a href="/tmj-arthroscopy">TMJ arthroscopy in Hyderabad</a> explains who it is offered to and why.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Swelling in front of the ear usually peaks at 48–72 hours and settles over one to two weeks.</li>
    <li>Your bite may feel slightly "off" on the operated side for a few days — this is common and normally temporary.</li>
    <li>Jaw exercises, started early and done regularly, matter as much as the procedure itself.</li>
    <li>Many people return to desk work in three to seven days; physical or voice-intensive work may need longer.</li>
    <li>Hard and chewy foods are reintroduced gradually, typically over four to six weeks.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="day-of-surgery">The day of surgery and the first 48 hours</h2>
<p>The procedure usually takes somewhere between half an hour and an hour and a half, depending on what needs to be done and whether one or both joints are treated. You wake up with small dressings, and sometimes a single stitch, in front of the ear. It is normal to notice:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Swelling and tightness</strong> in front of the ear and over the cheek, partly from the sterile fluid used to wash the joint.</li>
  <li><strong>A change in your bite.</strong> The back teeth on the operated side may not meet fully, because fluid and swelling hold the joint slightly apart.</li>
  <li><strong>Ear fullness or muffled hearing</strong> on that side, similar to the feeling during a flight.</li>
  <li><strong>Numbness</strong> of the skin near the ear or temple, and occasionally weakness when raising the eyebrow, from local anaesthetic or swelling around nearby nerves. This usually wears off within hours to days.</li>
</ul>
<p>What helps in these first two days:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Cold packs wrapped in a thin cloth, 15–20 minutes at a time, several times a day.</li>
  <li>Sleeping with your head raised on an extra pillow.</li>
  <li>Taking painkillers and other medicines exactly as prescribed, rather than waiting for pain to build.</li>
  <li>A liquid or very soft diet: soups, thin dal, buttermilk, smooth porridge, curd and mashed banana.</li>
  <li>Keeping the puncture sites clean and dry for as long as your team advises, usually about 24–48 hours.</li>
</ul>
<p>Do not drive, sign important documents or operate machinery for at least 24 hours after a general anaesthetic, and for longer if your painkillers make you drowsy. Arrange for someone to take you home and stay with you on the first night.</p>

<h2 id="week-one">Week 1: swelling settles, exercises begin</h2>
<p>By day three or four, swelling usually starts to go down, and your bite should feel more normal as the fluid is absorbed. Stiffness and a dull ache in the joint are expected. Most surgeons ask you to start gentle jaw exercises within the first day or two, because early movement helps prevent new scar tissue (adhesions) forming inside the joint.</p>
<p>Exercises in the first week are usually simple: slow opening and closing within a comfortable range, sliding the jaw from side to side, and moving it gently forward. Your surgeon or physiotherapist will give you a written programme with the number of repetitions and how often to do them. Mild discomfort during exercises is expected; sharp pain is a reason to stop and check with your team.</p>
<p>Many people return to office or desk-based work between day three and day seven. If your job involves a lot of talking — teaching, call centres, sales — you may tire more quickly in the first week or two. A first review is usually arranged at around one week to check healing, remove any stitch and measure your mouth opening.</p>

<h2 id="weeks-two-to-four">Weeks 2–4: building movement and diet</h2>
<p>This is when most of the visible progress happens. Swelling is usually gone or minimal, and the focus shifts to stretching the joint gradually and improving opening. Your programme may now include gentle assisted stretches, such as using light finger pressure, or a small stack of wooden spatulas between the front teeth, to hold the mouth open for a few seconds at a time.</p>
<p>Diet moves from very soft to "fork-tender" foods that need little chewing: idli, upma, khichdi, soft rice with dal, well-cooked vegetables, scrambled eggs, soft paneer, fish, and soft rotis torn into small pieces. Cut food small and chew on both sides if you can. Keep avoiding:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Hard or very chewy foods — sugarcane, corn on the cob, hard nuts, chikki, tough meat, crusty bread</li>
  <li>Biting into large items such as whole apples or thick sandwiches</li>
  <li>Chewing gum, paan or betel nut</li>
  <li>Very wide yawning (support your chin with your hand when you feel a yawn coming)</li>
</ul>
<p>If you were given a night-time splint before surgery, you will usually be asked to keep wearing it. Arthroscopy treats the inside of the joint, but it does not stop night-time clenching or grinding. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/tmj-splint-vs-surgery/">TMJ splints and when surgery is considered</a> explains why the two often work together.</p>

<?php gna_inline_cta('Recovering from TMJ arthroscopy and unsure whether your progress is on track?'); ?>

<h2 id="months-one-to-six">Months 1–6: getting back to normal</h2>
<p>From about four to six weeks, most people can return gradually to a normal diet, while still taking care with very hard or tough foods. Gym work and exercise can usually be resumed in stages after the first couple of weeks. Contact sports, where a blow to the jaw is possible, are typically delayed for longer — ask your surgeon for advice specific to you.</p>
<p>Pain and mouth opening often continue to improve for several months as the joint lining heals and the chewing muscles relax. Follow-up visits at around six weeks and again at three months are common, to measure opening, review pain, and decide whether to continue, reduce or stop the exercises and the splint.</p>
<p>It is worth being realistic about what a good outcome looks like. The aim of arthroscopy is a jaw that opens comfortably and works well for eating and speaking. A soft click may persist, and some people still have occasional flare-ups during stressful periods or after a long dental appointment. These are usually manageable with the same self-care measures you used before surgery.</p>

<h2 id="timeline-table">Recovery timeline at a glance</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Timeframe</th><th>What you may notice</th><th>What usually helps</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Days 0–2</td><td>Swelling, uneven bite, ear fullness, numbness near the ear</td><td>Cold packs, head raised, prescribed medicines, liquid or very soft diet</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Days 3–7</td><td>Swelling starting to settle, stiffness, dull ache</td><td>Gentle exercises as prescribed, soft diet, first review</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weeks 2–4</td><td>Opening improving, soreness after exercises</td><td>Progressive stretching, fork-tender foods, night splint if advised</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weeks 4–6</td><td>Most daily activities back to normal</td><td>Gradual return to a normal diet, continue exercises</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Months 3–6</td><td>Result becoming clear; occasional flare-ups possible</td><td>Long-term habits: managing clenching, posture and stress</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>These timings are typical, not fixed. Recovery tends to be slower if both joints were treated, if the joint had long-standing damage, or if pain had been present for many years before surgery.</p>

<h2 id="jaw-exercises">Why jaw exercises matter so much</h2>
<p>Arthroscopy can release adhesions, wash out inflammatory fluid and treat inflamed tissue, but the joint has to be moved regularly to keep the range gained in theatre. People who stop exercising early sometimes lose some of that opening. Structured physiotherapy after TMJ arthroscopy is widely recommended, although researchers have not agreed on a single ideal programme — so follow the plan your own team gives you.</p>
<p>A few practical tips:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Exercise little and often. Several short sessions spread through the day usually work better than one long session.</li>
  <li>Once the early swelling has gone, a warm compress for a few minutes before stretching can make movement easier.</li>
  <li>Keep a simple diary of your mouth opening — for example, how many finger-widths fit between your front teeth — so progress is easy to see at reviews.</li>
  <li>Never force a painful stretch. Steady, gradual gains are the goal.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="risks">Risks and possible complications</h2>
<p>TMJ arthroscopy is generally considered a low-risk procedure, but no operation is free of risk. Side effects that are common and usually temporary include swelling, a change in bite, ear fullness and numbness near the ear. Less common complications include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Infection at the puncture site or in the joint</li>
  <li>Weakness of the facial nerve branch that raises the eyebrow or closes the eye — usually temporary and recovering over weeks; lasting weakness is rare</li>
  <li>Injury to the ear canal or, rarely, changes in hearing</li>
  <li>Bleeding or bruising</li>
  <li>Scratching of the joint surfaces by the instruments</li>
  <li>Persistent pain or limited opening, which may need further treatment such as a repeat wash-out or, rarely, open joint surgery</li>
</ul>
<p>Your surgeon should explain these before you consent, including how likely each one is in your situation. Arthroscopy is one option among several, and simpler treatments such as <a href="/blogs/tmj-arthrocentesis-explained/">arthrocentesis, a needle wash-out of the joint</a>, may be considered first.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Contact your surgical team promptly if you notice:</strong> fever or chills; swelling that increases after day three instead of settling; pus or spreading redness at the puncture site; bleeding that does not stop with firm pressure; new weakness of the face or difficulty closing the eye; sudden hearing loss or discharge from the ear; or pain not controlled by your prescribed medicines. If you have difficulty breathing or swallowing, call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="when-to-call">When to contact your surgeon between visits</h2>
<p>Besides the warning signs above, it is reasonable to call for advice if your mouth opening is getting worse rather than better after the second week, if your bite still feels clearly different after a week, or if you are unsure how to do an exercise. Small problems are much easier to correct early.</p>
<p>If you have not had surgery yet and are still deciding, it may help to read about <a href="/blogs/evolution-of-tmj-arthroscopy/">how TMJ arthroscopy has developed over the years</a>, and to talk through with a specialist whether conservative care, arthrocentesis or arthroscopy suits your joint. Only an in-person examination, and imaging where needed, can answer that. Procedures are carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals in Hyderabad, and hospital-based procedures may be covered by health insurance, subject to your policy terms and pre-authorisation.</p>
